WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.036 --> 00:00:19.447
That night there was little change in our situation; we were still under five hundred feet of ice.

2
00:00:19.448 --> 00:00:27.447
During the night I woke up several times and found that we were slowly rising to the surface as the layer of ice above us grew thinner.

3
00:00:27.448 --> 00:00:33.019
Finally, at six o'clock in the morning, Captain Nemo came to the door of the gallery.

4
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:39.098
"The sea is open," was all he said before quickly departing, but that was enough for me.

5
00:00:39.099 --> 00:00:46.606
I went up to the platform, where the captain was looking at the water; it was still crowded with pieces of ice and moving icebergs.

6
00:00:46.607 --> 00:00:49.336
"Are we at the South Pole?" I asked.

7
00:00:49.337 --> 00:00:55.257
"I don't know," he replied. "I'll take our bearings at noon to determine our location."

8
00:00:55.258 --> 00:01:02.916
Captain Nemo invited my companions and me to join him when he set foot on land, but only Conseil and I were interested.

9
00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:09.320
Two crew members rowed us to shore in the small boat. Conseil was about to jump out when I held him back.

10
00:01:09.321 --> 00:01:16.313
"Sir," I said to Captain Nemo, "the honor of being first to set foot on land belongs to you."

11
00:01:16.314 --> 00:01:23.305
"Thank you," said the captain. He climbed out of the boat, and after a few minutes said to us, "Whenever you like . . ."

12
00:01:23.306 --> 00:01:28.226
We followed him onto the rocky shore, where we observed seals and penguins.

13
00:01:28.227 --> 00:01:35.988
Unfortunately, it was too foggy to see the sun and take our bearings, so we were still not sure about where we had landed.

14
00:01:35.989 --> 00:01:41.131
We returned to the submarine, disappointed but hoping to have better luck the next day.

15
00:01:41.132 --> 00:01:48.544
Unfortunately the following morning, our view of the sun was once again obscured, this time by heavy snow.

16
00:01:48.545 --> 00:01:55.798
Finally, on the third day, Captain Nemo led us toward a peak, where we watched the sun burst through the clouds.

17
00:01:55.799 --> 00:02:00.131
He made his observations and confirmed that we were at the South Pole.

18
00:02:00.132 --> 00:02:12.608
"I, Captain Nemo, have reached the South Pole today, March 21, 1868, and I take possession of this part of the globe," he said gravely.

19
00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:19.750
"In the name of which monarch?" I asked, remembering how an explorer usually claimed land on behalf of his ruler.

20
00:02:19.751 --> 00:02:26.679
"In my own name!" replied Captain Nemo, holding up a black flag bearing a gold N.

21
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:32.599
The next morning, at six o'clock, the crew of the Nautilus prepared to leave the South Pole.

22
00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:38.083
With its reservoirs filling with water, the submarine began its slow descent.

23
00:02:38.084 --> 00:02:44.496
At one thousand feet below the surface, it headed north at a steady 15 miles per hour.

24
00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:48.742
Toward night it was already floating under an immense iceberg.

25
00:02:48.743 --> 00:02:53.004
At three o'clock in the morning, I was awakened by a violent shock.

26
00:02:53.005 --> 00:02:57.893
I sat up in bed and was thrown into the middle of the room by another shock.

27
00:02:57.894 --> 00:03:02.885
The Nautilus , having struck something, had rebounded and hit something else.

28
00:03:02.886 --> 00:03:06.972
I rushed to the gallery, where I found the furniture overturned.

29
00:03:06.973 --> 00:03:12.059
I heard footsteps and excited voices, but Captain Nemo did not appear.

30
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.797
As I was leaving the gallery, Ned and Conseil entered.

31
00:03:15.798 --> 00:03:18.384
"What's going on?" I asked them.

32
00:03:18.385 --> 00:03:22.639
"That's what I came to ask you, Master," said Conseil.

33
00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.615
Ned scowled. "The Nautilus has run aground.

34
00:03:26.616 --> 00:03:32.282
Judging from the way it's lying, I doubt it'll right itself as it did in the Torres Strait."

35
00:03:32.283 --> 00:03:36.623
"So, do you think we're at the surface?" asked Conseil.

36
00:03:36.624 --> 00:03:38.632
"That's easy enough to find out."

37
00:03:38.633 --> 00:03:45.615
I consulted the instruments, and to my great surprise, I discovered that we were more than a thousand feet underwater.

38
00:03:45.616 --> 00:03:48.020
"We must speak to Captain Nemo."

39
00:03:48.021 --> 00:03:54.361
The three of us went in search of the captain, but when we couldn't find him, I decided he must be in the pilothouse.

40
00:03:54.362 --> 00:03:57.091
We returned to the gallery to wait for him.

41
00:03:57.092 --> 00:04:02.433
After about twenty minutes, the captain entered the room, but he took no notice of us.

42
00:04:02.434 --> 00:04:06.425
His normally emotionless face showed signs of unease.

43
00:04:06.426 --> 00:04:10.774
He watched the compass silently and then read the other instruments.

44
00:04:10.775 --> 00:04:16.266
I didn't want to interrupt him, but when he turned toward me, I decided to speak.

45
00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:22.496
"An incident, Captain?" I asked, using the phrase he'd preferred in the Torres Strait.

46
00:04:22.497 --> 00:04:25.322
"No, Professor, it's an accident."

47
00:04:25.323 --> 00:04:26.909
"Serious?"'

48
00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:33.242
"Perhaps," he replied. "We've run aground underwater, but we aren't in immediate danger."

49
00:04:33.243 --> 00:04:36.917
"May I ask, sir, what caused this accident?"

50
00:04:36.918 --> 00:04:42.655
"An enormous block of ice, an entire iceberg, turned over and slid under the Nautilus ."

51
00:04:42.656 --> 00:04:50.457
Captain Nemo said that the submarine was now emptying its reservoirs in an attempt to right itself, but the block of ice was rising with it.

52
00:04:50.458 --> 00:04:54.473
He explained this while never taking his eyes from the instruments.

53
00:04:54.474 --> 00:05:01.647
Suddenly we felt a small movement below us; evidently the submarine was righting itself slightly.

54
00:05:01.648 --> 00:05:11.298
Ten long minutes passed while we watched the pictures on the wall slowly return to their normal position and the floor become horizontal under our feet.

55
00:05:11.299 --> 00:05:14.124
"Are we floating again?" I asked.

56
00:05:14.125 --> 00:05:18.529
"Of course," replied the captain, "but we're still underwater."

57
00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:26.370
I looked through the windows and saw that a dazzling wall of ice rose about ten yards away from either side of the Nautilus .

58
00:05:26.371 --> 00:05:29.537
There was also ice above and below us.

59
00:05:29.538 --> 00:05:34.362
We were imprisoned in a perfect tunnel of ice filled with water.

60
00:05:34.363 --> 00:05:37.608
But I decided it would be easy to escape the tunnel.

61
00:05:37.609 --> 00:05:42.862
The submarine could go either forward or backward and then dive under the iceberg.

62
00:05:42.863 --> 00:05:47.362
Captain Nemo left us, and I assumed he returned to the pilothouse.

63
00:05:47.363 --> 00:05:54.187
My companions and I stayed at the gallery windows, dazzled by the submarine's light sparkling on the ice.

64
00:05:54.188 --> 00:06:01.505
At five in the morning, we felt a shock at the vessel's bow; its battering ram had obviously struck a block of ice.

65
00:06:01.506 --> 00:06:08.330
"We must go in reverse now, and see if we can find another way out," I said, trying to appear cheerful.

66
00:06:08.331 --> 00:06:14.346
"That'll take hours more," grumbled Ned. "How long are we going to be stuck here?"

67
00:06:14.347 --> 00:06:18.822
I had no idea, so I went to the library to calm my nerves.

68
00:06:18.823 --> 00:06:22.076
I threw myself on a couch and tried to read a book.

69
00:06:22.077 --> 00:06:26.148
Fifteen minutes later Conseil and Ned entered the room.

70
00:06:26.149 --> 00:06:29.989
"Is that an interesting book, Master?" asked Conseil.

71
00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.386
"Very interesting," I replied.

72
00:06:32.387 --> 00:06:36.973
"Well, it should be." Conseil laughed. "It's the one you wrote!"

73
00:06:36.974 --> 00:06:42.219
I slammed the book shut, and the two of them turned to go. I stood up.

74
00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:47.727
"Let's return to the gallery," I said. "We should stay together until we're out of this predicament."

75
00:06:47.728 --> 00:06:50.886
"As you wish, Master," replied Conseil.

76
00:06:50.887 --> 00:06:57.553
Several hours passed while the Nautilus moved backward at a good rate of speed, and we kept an eye on the instruments.

77
00:06:57.554 --> 00:07:02.711
It was almost 8:30 when we felt another shock, this time at the stern.

78
00:07:02.712 --> 00:07:06.370
I turned pale and seized Conseil's hand.

79
00:07:06.371 --> 00:07:09.370
The three of us exchanged troubled glances.

80
00:07:09.371 --> 00:07:12.457
At this point Captain Nemo entered the room.

81
00:07:12.458 --> 00:07:17.455
"The iceberg has shifted and closed up every outlet," he announced.

82
00:07:17.456 --> 00:07:21.837
Once again we were trapped in the ice!
